# TAGS & RELEASES
Tag = it notihing but marking at particular commit of it's release
exp: commit id 1) qwer23
              2) bfrg5 --> relese point at this tag,here we can tag like v1.0
               3)bhyt7 
                4) yut6798 --> second release, we can mark tag like v2.0

******DIFFERENT TYPES OF TAGS ************
 LIGHTWEIGHT TAGS == these tags are just like bookmark, doesn't save metadat 
  # git tag v1.0

ANNOTATED TAGS = stores extra info like tagger name, name, email,tag message & we can write a message to this tag

      # git tag -a v1.0 -m "message"

***VIEWING TAGS******
  #git tag 
  # git tag -l or git tag --list

*******SEE SPECIFIC TAG *********
git show v1.0

************DIFF B/W TAGS ***************
# git diff tag1 tag2
**************DELETE TAG LOCALLY & REMOTE ******************
# git tag -d v1.0 = locally
# git push origin --delete v1.0 = remote

**************CREATE A TAG FOR OLDER COMMIT  ************
# git tag v1.0 <cmt.id> =ligtweight
# git tag -a v1.0 -m "message" <cmt.id> = annotated tag

************ FORCE TAG ************
you have a tag v1.0 pointing to an old commit, but you want it to point to the latest commit
# git tag -f v1.0  => tags to new commit id 
# git tag -f v1.0 <cmt.id> for specific commit 
*************FOR ANNOTATED TAG*********
# git tag -a v1.0 -f -m "message" &  -m "message" <cmt.id> for specfic
or use HEAD~1 at the end for last second commit 

*********PUSH TAGS ***********
# git push origin v1.0
# git push origin --all (to push all the tags)
********PATTERN SEARCH*************
# git tag -list "v1.*" (displays all the tags starts with v1)







